course: dictionary
lesson: 14714
title: Formal words: procure
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound grown-up and stiff. Procure is one of them. It means to get or obtain something, often with some effort. «The explorers procured a map from the old innkeeper.» They did not just find the map. They had to work to get it.
explain: Look at the words around procure to see what kind of getting it means. «It took hours to procure enough food for the long journey.» The words hours and enough tell you that getting the food was hard work. Ask yourself: could I swap in simpler words like get or find? If the sentence still makes sense, you have the meaning.
explain: Procure has a second meaning, used in business and government. When a company or a government procures things, it buys them in an official way. «The school procures paper and pencils for every class.» Here the school is not just getting things. It is buying them officially, through its proper system.
fact: Procure means to get or obtain something, often with some effort.
fact: «Procure» is a formal word, mostly used in writing and exams.
fact: When a company or government procures supplies, it buys them officially.
fact: Common phrases include procure supplies, procure food and procure funds.
fact: Synonyms for procure include obtain and acquire, but procure often suggests effort or a proper process.
exercise: «They procured food for the journey.» What does procured mean here? | *Got, often with some effort | Dropped on the way | Ate all of | Threw away
exercise: The office manager must procure paper for the whole office. What does procure mean in this sentence? | Hide it in a drawer | Lose it on the bus | *Buy it officially | Give it back to the shop
exercise: Find the word that is closest in meaning to procure. | *obtain | forget | refuse | ignore
test: «The rescue team procured blankets and water for the villagers.» What does procured suggest? | Left behind by accident | Broke them | Gave them away for free | *Got them, probably with some effort
test: The government procures medicine for hospitals across the country. Why would it procure medicine? | To sell it at the seaside | To throw it away | *To buy it officially for the hospitals | To hide it from doctors
exam: «The team had to travel for days to procure the rare seeds.» Which phrase is the best clue to the meaning of procure? | travel for days | to the | rare seeds | the team | *had to travel for days
